INTENTIONAL, and ruled by the operator rather than by a dispatch. Under `-k`
with a prerequisite nothing can make, GNU Make 4.4.1 builds the three objects it
can and only then abandons the goal; Ronin refuses at graph-load time, because
the goal provably cannot complete, and builds none of them. Same exit status,
three fewer files, and none of the three was going to be used.

Operator decision, Brendan, 2026-08-24, on
docs/make-oracle-divergences.md section 10: "Ronin superior. Accepted
divergence." Asked whether to keep it, he answered KEEP — fail fast is right,
and refusing at graph-load when the goal provably cannot complete beats doing
work that gets thrown away.

Not a defect to be repaired. This case is here so that a change which starts
building the three objects reopens that decision instead of passing quietly:
`make_port` fails a recorded divergence that has been "fixed" as loudly as it
fails an unrecorded one. Owner:
make-keep-going-builds-what-it-can-past-an-unmakeable-prerequisite.
